00:04:27.099 --> 00:04:33.039
Not long before Kris was found in his roughed-up
state outside the only Ikea that goes on forever,

00:04:33.039 --> 00:04:37.800
he was working at a different branch, one
that was much smaller on the inside.

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:43.440
Kris was the manager of the store, and widely
regarded, by his employees, to be an awful

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:44.440
person.

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:48.169
Nobody knew if he had always been that way,
or if instilling him in a position of power

00:04:48.169 --> 00:04:53.460
over other retail workers had somehow caused
him to develop a bit of a god complex.

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:58.889
Either way, there was little he ever did to
invoke much comradery with his colleagues.

00:04:58.889 --> 00:05:02.370
Whenever any of the store’s workers were
on their breaks – which they usually had

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:08.000
to remind Kris were permitted by law, and
not optional – conversation often turned

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:11.210
to their collective poor opinions of the manager.

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:16.710
He’d been given the job out of pretty blatant
nepotism; it was well-known how cosy Kris

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:20.560
was with the regional manager of all the Ikea
stores in the area.

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:25.110
On top of that, Kris had a pretty nasty habit
of trying to force his employees to stay behind

00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:29.650
after their scheduled shifts had finished,
while also refusing to pay them for overtime.

00:05:29.650 --> 00:05:35.020
You see, it was his own mismanagement of the
work rota that left the store understaffed

00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:37.120
at crucial moments of the day.

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:42.080
The solution, you would think, should be obvious:
hiring a few more new staff to help with the

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:46.220
workload and to start their shifts at times
when others were about to clock out for the

00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:47.220
day.

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:51.889
Ah, but new employees wouldn’t work for
free, their wages would come out of the store’s

00:05:51.889 --> 00:05:52.889
budget.

00:05:52.889 --> 00:05:55.729
And why would Kris want to take on new workers?

00:05:55.729 --> 00:06:00.570
As the person in charge of payroll, he could
help himself to that money, adding the wages

00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:07.130
of prospective colleagues as a bonus on top
of his own unjustifiably exorbitant salary.

00:06:07.130 --> 00:06:11.610
Perhaps the worst part about having Kris as
a manager was that he seemed to get away with

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:12.620
murder.

00:06:12.620 --> 00:06:17.270
As long as the store kept making a profit,
then the management above him didn’t care

00:06:17.270 --> 00:06:22.020
how badly he treated his employees or flouted
the rules for his own gain.

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:27.630
But it was those same colleagues who had suffered
and been scorned by Kris’ behaviour who

00:06:27.630 --> 00:06:30.349
had finally had enough.

00:06:30.349 --> 00:06:35.889
Retail work is hard, having to deal with rude
and impolite customers on a day to day basis,

00:06:35.889 --> 00:06:41.870
working long hours of uninteresting and repetitive
labour, all for a pay check that only just

00:06:41.870 --> 00:06:42.990
covered rent.

00:06:42.990 --> 00:06:48.650
Like we said, it’s a hard line of work,
and the people in it deserve the utmost respect.

00:06:48.650 --> 00:06:53.770
But you throw a megalomaniacal manager into
the mix, and you’ve got a ticking time bomb

00:06:53.770 --> 00:06:58.360
on your hands, one that’ll sooner or later
explode.

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:02.570
It was witnessing Kris blatantly framing a
younger member of staff that finally triggered

00:07:02.570 --> 00:07:03.699
detonation.

00:07:03.699 --> 00:07:07.949
The kid had only been a student, working at
Ikea all through his weekends to make enough

00:07:07.949 --> 00:07:09.889
money to pay his bills.

00:07:09.889 --> 00:07:14.949
Kris saw this less experienced employee not
as a human being doing his best, but a chance

00:07:14.949 --> 00:07:17.970
to make a quick buck by breaking the rules.

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:22.159
The manager started stealing cash from the
register, then blamed it on his young staff

00:07:22.159 --> 00:07:26.879
member when the losses started to show on
the store’s monthly financial reports.

00:07:26.879 --> 00:07:31.000
Seeing that for themselves, the other members
of staff knew it was the manager’s responsibility

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:35.689
to empty the registers regularly, so they
didn’t have too much cash in them in case

00:07:35.689 --> 00:07:37.199
of a robbery.

00:07:37.199 --> 00:07:41.949
Given how much they all despised Kris, it
didn’t take much effort for the entire workforce

00:07:41.949 --> 00:07:45.819
to organise a walk-out, halfway through the
working day.

00:07:45.819 --> 00:07:50.560
Outraged, Kris had tried calling any colleagues
that were off-shift, barking at them to come

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:52.569
in and work on their days off.

00:07:52.569 --> 00:07:54.659
All of them said no.

00:07:54.659 --> 00:08:00.250
The thing is, when you work in retail, complaints
to upper management never seem to go anywhere.

00:08:00.250 --> 00:08:04.509
Like we said, if the store’s making profit,
that’s all they care about.

00:08:04.509 --> 00:08:09.259
But seeing such drastic action from the staff
of the store he was supposed to be running,

00:08:09.259 --> 00:08:12.930
the Ikea higher-ups came down on Kris like
a hammer.

00:08:12.930 --> 00:08:18.409
They instructed that he be sent to another
Ikea outlet for a whole afternoon, to receive

00:08:18.409 --> 00:08:21.689
some mandatory interpersonal training.

00:08:21.689 --> 00:08:26.289
Little did anyone realize that this was the
last anyone would ever see of him – not

00:08:26.289 --> 00:08:30.580
that his old staff was sad to hear he had
disappeared.

00:08:30.580 --> 00:08:34.920
Arriving at what looked like an ordinary Ikea,
Kris parked his car outside.

00:08:34.920 --> 00:08:40.390
The place seemed deserted, no customers filing
in and out of the front doors, the only other

00:08:40.390 --> 00:08:45.200
vehicles in the parking lot looking like they
had just been abandoned by their owners.

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:47.410
The manager scoffed to himself.

00:08:47.410 --> 00:08:51.800
These people running this store were going
to teach him how to do his job?

00:08:51.800 --> 00:08:55.910
Indignantly, he clipped his name tag on and
stepped out of his car, marching across the

00:08:55.910 --> 00:08:57.790
asphalt towards the entrance.

00:08:57.790 --> 00:09:03.940
The automatic doors gently slid open, shutting
smoothly behind Kris as he stepped inside.

00:09:03.940 --> 00:09:08.510
The whole store was quiet, barely a peep from
anywhere.

00:09:08.510 --> 00:09:13.670
The high ceiling of the Ikea store he so poorly
managed carried a lot of noise and made any

00:09:13.670 --> 00:09:15.850
sound echo throughout the building.

00:09:15.850 --> 00:09:20.330
The squeaking of shoes against the vinyl floors,
the bustle of customers, the electronic beep

00:09:20.330 --> 00:09:25.020
of cash registers, and the low, dulled music
playing over the intercom.

00:09:25.020 --> 00:09:26.530
But there was none of that here.

00:09:26.530 --> 00:09:31.690
A deathly silence filled the aisles of flat
pack furniture, display models of Hemneses

00:09:31.690 --> 00:09:36.340
and Trysils standing as still and foreboding
as gravestones.

00:09:36.340 --> 00:09:41.050
Suddenly, the sound of movement reverberated
from between one row of aisles.

00:09:41.050 --> 00:09:45.560
Kris began marching towards its source, expecting
to find an employee who could point him in

00:09:45.560 --> 00:09:49.820
the direction of the office where his training
was supposed to be conducted.

00:09:49.820 --> 00:09:55.170
Sure enough, within a few steps, Kris spotted
a figure in a familiar bright yellow t-shirt

00:09:55.170 --> 00:10:00.650
and blue pants; the same uniform his Ikea
underlings wore at his store.

00:10:00.650 --> 00:10:05.250
“Excuse me,” he called as he strode towards
the employee, shoes clomping against the floor

00:10:05.250 --> 00:10:06.250
with every step.

00:10:06.250 --> 00:10:09.220
“You need to tell me where your manager
is, I’m from the store across town.

00:10:09.220 --> 00:10:11.620
Here for some pointless training seminar.”

00:10:11.620 --> 00:10:16.530
Kris hadn’t waited until he was close enough
to engage in pleasant conversation, instead

00:10:16.530 --> 00:10:18.650
making his demands as he approached.

00:10:18.650 --> 00:10:23.060
The figure in the Ikea uniform, however, didn’t
turn to face him.

00:10:23.060 --> 00:10:24.060
“Hey!

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:25.100
I’m talking to you!”

00:10:25.100 --> 00:10:26.100
Kris shouted.

00:10:26.100 --> 00:10:27.240
“You deaf or something?”

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:31.620
There was a pause, telling Kris that what
happened next wasn’t in response to what

00:10:31.620 --> 00:10:35.220
he said; although that didn’t make it any
less frightening.

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:38.990
The figure, who had been hunched over near
the floor, suddenly drew itself up to its

00:10:38.990 --> 00:10:40.160
full height.

00:10:40.160 --> 00:10:44.600
It towered over Kris, it must have measured
close to seven whole feet.

00:10:44.600 --> 00:10:49.630
As it turned, the malignant manager saw its
face… or that it didn’t have one.

00:10:49.630 --> 00:10:55.820
No eyes, no mouth, nothing but a smooth surface
without any discernible features.

00:10:55.820 --> 00:11:02.010
Its arms were elongated, huge, claw-like hands
hanging down to the creature’s knees.

00:11:02.010 --> 00:11:04.970
Being a coward, Kris turned and ran immediately.

00:11:04.970 --> 00:11:09.351
Confrontation was another area his employees
had noticed he was sorely lacking in; whenever

00:11:09.351 --> 00:11:13.510
faced with a rude and cantankerous customer,
Kris would never defend his colleagues or

00:11:13.510 --> 00:11:14.520
their actions.

00:11:14.520 --> 00:11:19.940
Instead, he would suck up and brownnose to
customers who were clearly in the wrong, essentially

00:11:19.940 --> 00:11:22.760
rolling over the minute anyone raised their
voice.

00:11:22.760 --> 00:11:28.250
So, now confronted with a lumbering, faceless
entity, his self-preservation instincts were

00:11:28.250 --> 00:11:33.310
kicked into overdrive, and he raced deeper
into the store.

00:11:33.310 --> 00:11:37.820
Not exactly in the best shape, he slowed to
a halt, wheezing as he tried to catch his

00:11:37.820 --> 00:11:38.820
breath.

00:11:38.820 --> 00:11:43.280
That was when, looking around, Kris noticed
just how long the aisle seemed.

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:46.811
He was sure he would still be able to see
the front door from here, yet couldn’t spot

00:11:46.811 --> 00:11:52.030
the entrance anywhere from where he was currently
standing, panting like a thirsty dog.

00:11:52.030 --> 00:11:56.840
He looked over his shoulder; there was no
sign of the Staff member he’d encountered.

00:11:56.840 --> 00:12:00.740
Little did Kris know that it had lumbered
off in the opposite direction the second he

00:12:00.740 --> 00:12:03.770
had run away from it, hardly paying him any
mind…

00:12:03.770 --> 00:12:06.600
at least, while the lights were still on.

00:12:06.600 --> 00:12:11.070
Unable to find the entrance, or even a fire
door he could escape through, it didn’t

00:12:11.070 --> 00:12:17.080
take long for the lights inside the Infinite
Ikea to dim, marking the start of the night

00:12:17.080 --> 00:12:18.080
cycle.

00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:21.150
Something about it being darker made the sounds
of shuffling even more noticeable…

00:12:21.150 --> 00:12:24.820
there were more than one of those faceless,
uniformed creatures.

00:12:24.820 --> 00:12:29.850
Trembling, Kris climbed inside a Kleppstad,
a wardrobe with a sliding door.

00:12:29.850 --> 00:12:34.390
Barely able to sleep with his knees tucked
up under his chin, he spent the entire night

00:12:34.390 --> 00:12:37.550
inside the cramped, confined space.

00:12:37.550 --> 00:12:41.230
Every sound of movement from outside brought
with it the mental image of one of the Staff

00:12:41.230 --> 00:12:45.520
sliding open the outer door and reaching down
to grab him.

00:12:45.520 --> 00:12:50.410
The whole sleepless while, Kris couldn’t
help but think about what he could have possibly

00:12:50.410 --> 00:12:53.810
done to deserve this cruel fate.

00:12:53.810 --> 00:12:57.640
The next morning, the lights came back on
and started to bleed through the seams of

00:12:57.640 --> 00:13:02.710
the flat pack wardrobe, waking Kris from what
little restless sleep he had gotten.

00:13:02.710 --> 00:13:06.510
He paused, unsure if he should venture out
in case he came across another one of the

00:13:06.510 --> 00:13:07.510
Staff.

00:13:07.510 --> 00:13:11.750
Suddenly, he heard the sound of voices, muted
and muffled by the door of the Kleppstad,

00:13:11.750 --> 00:13:14.270
but definitely real human voices.

00:13:14.270 --> 00:13:15.930
Had it all been some horrible nightmare?

00:13:15.930 --> 00:13:20.630
Had he fallen asleep at work and been stuffed
into a wardrobe by his ungrateful employees?

00:13:20.630 --> 00:13:27.540
Oh, now he had them, he would sue every last
one of them out of their jobs.

00:13:27.540 --> 00:13:31.320
Sliding the door open and tumbling out of
the Kleppstad, Kris’ found himself lying

00:13:31.320 --> 00:13:35.590
face down on the store floor, his legs too
cramped to move.

00:13:35.590 --> 00:13:39.990
Just as he tried to will them into moving
again, a voice called out to him.

00:13:39.990 --> 00:13:40.990
“Hey, mister!”

00:13:40.990 --> 00:13:41.990
it said.

00:13:41.990 --> 00:13:42.990
“You okay there?”

00:13:42.990 --> 00:13:46.640
A trio of footsteps raced over to where he
was, and before he could get good look at

00:13:46.640 --> 00:13:50.710
who they belonged to, hands were pulling Kris
up off the floor.

00:13:50.710 --> 00:13:55.330
He looked at his rescuers, bemused to see
other people in this endless void of affordable

00:13:55.330 --> 00:13:56.460
home wear.

00:13:56.460 --> 00:14:00.890
They were two men and a woman, each in disheveled
clothing that looked like they had been wearing

00:14:00.890 --> 00:14:02.650
the same thing for years.

00:14:02.650 --> 00:14:05.900
“Easy there, fella,” one of the men spoke.

00:14:05.900 --> 00:14:07.570
“Name’s Buster, which settlement you from?”

00:14:07.570 --> 00:14:08.570
“What…

00:14:08.570 --> 00:14:10.490
what do you mean by ‘settlement’?”

00:14:10.490 --> 00:14:11.490
Kris asked, confused.

00:14:11.490 --> 00:14:15.090
“Looks like we got another newbie,” the
woman chuckled dryly.

00:14:15.090 --> 00:14:16.720
“When did you get here?”

00:14:16.720 --> 00:14:19.840
“Yesterday, I think,” the former manager
answered.

00:14:19.840 --> 00:14:23.050
“Time can be a bit screwy in here,” the
second man chimed in.

00:14:23.050 --> 00:14:24.230
“I’m Nolan.

00:14:24.230 --> 00:14:25.230
This here is Janine.

00:14:25.230 --> 00:14:26.520
You got a name?”

00:14:26.520 --> 00:14:27.710
“Kris,” he nodded.

00:14:27.710 --> 00:14:28.890
“You hungry, Kris?”

00:14:28.890 --> 00:14:29.890
Buster offered.

00:14:29.890 --> 00:14:34.390
The three survivors led Kris through the winding
expanse of the Ikea without an end, navigating

00:14:34.390 --> 00:14:38.860
the identical aisles with the expertise of
people that had been here long enough to know

00:14:38.860 --> 00:14:39.860
their way around.

00:14:39.860 --> 00:14:44.340
In what felt like the tiniest fraction of
the time that he had been lost the day before,

00:14:44.340 --> 00:14:46.610
they brought Kris to the food court.

00:14:46.610 --> 00:14:51.640
It was bustling with activity, more so than
the one at his store, with people patiently

00:14:51.640 --> 00:14:55.320
lining up to take their fill of food from
the Ikea menu.

00:14:55.320 --> 00:14:59.210
After joining the queue, Buster returned and
handed Kris a plate of Swedish meatballs with

00:14:59.210 --> 00:15:03.170
mashed potato, peas, cream sauce, and lingonberry
jam.

00:15:03.170 --> 00:15:07.670
So pleased to see food again, Kris began to
gluttonously devour the whole plate while

00:15:07.670 --> 00:15:12.740
the others explained some of what was going
on here; although he was barely listening.

00:15:12.740 --> 00:15:16.831
According to Janine, who had been there the
longest, the inside of this Ikea was like

00:15:16.831 --> 00:15:22.270
an endless maze, there was no clear way out,
even when retracing steps back the way one

00:15:22.270 --> 00:15:23.890
first entered.

00:15:23.890 --> 00:15:27.790
Nolan then weighed in and explained that there
were pockets of people who had survived this

00:15:27.790 --> 00:15:32.510
long inside SCP-3008 by forming their own
little communities.

00:15:32.510 --> 00:15:36.430
Thanks to the sheer amount of space within
the store, these settlements were almost the

00:15:36.430 --> 00:15:41.640
size of small townships, built using whatever
furniture, appliances, and other materials

00:15:41.640 --> 00:15:44.010
the other survivors had been able to scrounge
up.

00:15:44.010 --> 00:15:45.950
“How come you don’t run out of food?”

00:15:45.950 --> 00:15:46.950
Kris interjected.

00:15:46.950 --> 00:15:51.340
“It replenishes every day, man,” Buster
replied, gesturing to the food court around

00:15:51.340 --> 00:15:52.340
them.

00:15:52.340 --> 00:15:53.480
“Sure, it’s not ideal if you aren’t
a huge fan of Ikea food.

00:15:53.480 --> 00:15:55.691
But you get used to it.

00:15:55.691 --> 00:15:57.350
Could be worse, all things considered.”

00:15:57.350 --> 00:15:59.750
“Then what the hell do you all do for money?”

00:15:59.750 --> 00:16:01.150
the suborn manager demanded.

00:16:01.150 --> 00:16:03.350
“Look around you,” Janine scoffed.

00:16:03.350 --> 00:16:04.860
“We don’t need it in here.

00:16:04.860 --> 00:16:08.090
We’re cut off from the rest of the world,
stuff like money hardly makes a difference.

00:16:08.090 --> 00:16:11.530
It can’t get us home, or keep us fed, so
what’s the point?”

00:16:11.530 --> 00:16:16.610
Kris sighed, evidently frustrated at the prospect
of living inside an Ikea yet not having the

00:16:16.610 --> 00:16:18.870
opportunity for monetary gain.

00:16:18.870 --> 00:16:21.520
“So, who’s in charge?” he asked.

00:16:21.520 --> 00:16:23.130
“Who runs this place?”

00:16:23.130 --> 00:16:24.760
“No one,” Nolan shrugged.

00:16:24.760 --> 00:16:27.530
“The settlements all work together, help
each other out if needed.

00:16:27.530 --> 00:16:30.630
But we don’t have any singular person running
things.”

00:16:30.630 --> 00:16:35.500
Hearing that out loud caused a twisted idea
to form in Kris’ balding head.

00:16:35.500 --> 00:16:39.380
Asking to be escorted by Buster to the right
aisle, he started gathering up what he needed

00:16:39.380 --> 00:16:41.390
to make a rudimentary crown.

00:16:41.390 --> 00:16:42.960
“What’s that for?”

00:16:42.960 --> 00:16:45.330
Buster asked, a little concerned about what
Kris was planning.

00:16:45.330 --> 00:16:49.400
“Oh, you wouldn’t understand,” he replied,
not looking up from applying hot glue.

00:16:49.400 --> 00:16:53.180
“Don’t worry, I’ll explain it in nice,
simple terms when I’m ready.”

00:16:53.180 --> 00:16:56.680
That night, despite being welcomed into the
trio’s town of ‘Lighting’ with open

00:16:56.680 --> 00:17:01.510
arms, Kris slipped back to the food court
while the other settlers were sleeping.

00:17:01.510 --> 00:17:07.079
Carefully, he avoided the lurking Staff, and
their droned calls of ‘The store is now

00:17:07.079 --> 00:17:09.789
closed, please exit the building’.

00:17:09.789 --> 00:17:14.049
Reaching his destination, the malicious manager
lay in wait for the next morning, hiding out

00:17:14.049 --> 00:17:17.000
underneath the counter where the food was
served.

00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:21.079
The next morning, the collected survivors
of the Infinite Ikea were met with an unfamiliar

00:17:21.079 --> 00:17:23.280
sight as they approached the food court.

00:17:23.280 --> 00:17:27.879
All the food had gone, it had still replenished
overnight like it usually did, but someone

00:17:27.879 --> 00:17:31.440
had gathered it up before anyone had a chance
to eat.

00:17:31.440 --> 00:17:35.379
Stood atop the counter with a smug look on
his face, Kris made a show of placing his

00:17:35.379 --> 00:17:37.039
crown over his head.

00:17:37.039 --> 00:17:39.070
“Morning everyone!” he announced.

00:17:39.070 --> 00:17:42.110
“From today, things are going to be a little
different around here.

00:17:42.110 --> 00:17:46.450
But I’m sure we’ll all be able to work
together like one big, happy family.”

00:17:46.450 --> 00:17:51.940
Holding the gathered SCP-3008 residents as
his literally captive audience, all of them

00:17:51.940 --> 00:17:56.309
hungry and irritable, Kris outlined what he
had determined to be the best system possible

00:17:56.309 --> 00:17:59.429
for how things should be run inside the endless
store.

00:17:59.429 --> 00:18:04.549
“I will keep all the food secure and safe,
and we’re going to start rationing it out,”

00:18:04.549 --> 00:18:05.549
he explained.

00:18:05.549 --> 00:18:06.549
“What for?”

00:18:06.549 --> 00:18:07.600
a voice shouted from the crowd.

00:18:07.600 --> 00:18:09.350
“It reappears every night anyway!”

00:18:09.350 --> 00:18:13.639
“Well, here’s what we’re going to do,
if you’ll just shut up and let me finish,”

00:18:13.639 --> 00:18:17.340
Kris replied calmly, but with a condescending
tone of superiority.

00:18:17.340 --> 00:18:21.639
According to Kris’ grand scheme, every day
each settlement would be allocated what he

00:18:21.639 --> 00:18:26.280
deemed to be an acceptable amount of rationed
food to feed its population of survivors.

00:18:26.280 --> 00:18:29.220
Then, he made an offer to anyone who was willing
to help him.

00:18:29.220 --> 00:18:33.800
If someone provided their services in collecting
the food each morning and distributing the

00:18:33.800 --> 00:18:38.529
rationed amount, then Kris would allow them
to take an extra portion of rations just for

00:18:38.529 --> 00:18:39.529
themselves.

00:18:39.529 --> 00:18:43.519
“You see now?” he asked the crowd, oblivious
to how many of them were scowling at him.

00:18:43.519 --> 00:18:47.070
“Anyone that wants to help, they get rewarded
with a little bit extra.

00:18:47.070 --> 00:18:48.070
Call it a bonus!”

00:18:48.070 --> 00:18:50.070
“What if we don’t want to help you?”

00:18:50.070 --> 00:18:51.419
another onlooker called.

00:18:51.419 --> 00:18:53.720
“That’s fine by me,” Kris sneered back.

00:18:53.720 --> 00:18:55.320
“More for those who want it!”

00:18:55.320 --> 00:18:58.580
“I think, what they mean is,” Janine shouted
from the group.

00:18:58.580 --> 00:19:02.399
“What if we don’t want you to implement
this ridiculous system at all?

00:19:02.399 --> 00:19:06.190
What’s to stop us just taking the food we
need every morning like we did before you

00:19:06.190 --> 00:19:07.279
showed up?”

00:19:07.279 --> 00:19:08.809
“Typical,” he tutted.

00:19:08.809 --> 00:19:09.830
“How lazy.

00:19:09.830 --> 00:19:13.370
You want the privilege of having more, then
you should work to earn it!

00:19:13.370 --> 00:19:18.179
And as for your silly notion of just taking,
I’m going to be here every morning.

00:19:18.179 --> 00:19:22.320
I’ll get to decide who eats and who doesn’t,
and how much they deserve.

00:19:22.320 --> 00:19:26.919
And if anyone wants to take more than they
deserve, without offering their help in return,

00:19:26.919 --> 00:19:29.809
then they’ll get half of the rations they
would get.

00:19:29.809 --> 00:19:31.129
And so will their settlement.

00:19:31.129 --> 00:19:37.850
So, with those queries out of the way, allow
me to introduce myself as your new store manager!”

00:19:37.850 --> 00:19:42.480
When they recovered Kris’ body outside the
entrance to SCP-3008, the Foundation had no

00:19:42.480 --> 00:19:46.340
way of knowing exactly who had beaten him
up so badly.

00:19:46.340 --> 00:19:51.190
The other survivors inside the Infinite Ikea
might have done it themselves, while overthrowing

00:19:51.190 --> 00:19:53.700
his short-lived stint as manager.

00:19:53.700 --> 00:19:58.450
Or they might have decided not to lower themselves
to that level, and instead shunned him from

00:19:58.450 --> 00:20:02.240
entering their settlements, leaving him to
fend for himself.

00:20:02.240 --> 00:20:06.210
With the Staff around every corner, and their
history of turning aggressive towards humans

00:20:06.210 --> 00:20:09.809
come nightfall, Kris’ chances of survival
were slim.

00:20:09.809 --> 00:20:12.990
The only real clue was the note that had been
found with him, which read:

00:20:12.990 --> 00:20:14.700
‘You can have him back.

00:20:14.700 --> 00:20:15.830
We don’t want him.’

00:20:15.830 --> 00:20:19.640
Now, if you aren’t quite ready to checkout
of the Infinite Ikea, then you should go and

00:20:19.640 --> 00:20:25.539
check out “LIFE IN THE ENDLESS IKEA” for
another tale of a trapped soul in SCP-3008.

00:20:25.539 --> 00:20:29.220
And then, if you ever want suggestions on
what to do if you find yourself in the same

00:20:29.220 --> 00:20:34.370
predicament, then “HOW TO ACTUALLY BEAT
SCP-3008 THE INFINITE IKEA” is the only

00:20:34.370 --> 00:20:35.491
survival guide you’ll ever need!

